# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Heap-debug profile. Layered ON TOP of sdkconfig.defaults — later files win:
#
#   idf.py -B build_heapdbg -D SIP_TRANSPORT=eth \
#          -DSDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS="sdkconfig.defaults;sdkconfig.defaults.heap_debug" \
#          build
#
# This is a BUG-FINDING build, not a shipping one. Do not cut a release from it.
#
# Why it exists: the shipped default enables HEAP_POISONING_LIGHT, which only
# verifies an allocation's canaries when it is freed. That catches overflow and
# double-free, but it cannot see a read of memory that was never initialised, or
# a use-after-free that never reaches another free().
#
# We shipped exactly that first class of bug: draw_qr() in main/ui/ui.cpp used to
# return before lv_canvas_fill_bg() when qrcode_initText failed, painting the
# contents of a freshly malloc'd 19,602-byte buffer to the display. Uninitialised
# heap looks like plausible noise, so it rendered as a QR-ish smear that nobody
# questioned. COMPREHENSIVE fills fresh allocations with 0xCE and freed ones with
# 0xFE, which would have made that a flat, obviously-wrong pattern the first time
# anyone looked at the screen.
#
# Cost, and why it is not the default: COMPREHENSIVE memsets on every allocation
# AND every free. That is a real per-call cost on a device whose media path is
# already latency-sensitive, and it is exactly the wrong trade on the
# 4 MB/no-PSRAM profile in sdkconfig.defaults.esp32_constrained.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

CONFIG_HEAP_POISONING_COMPREHENSIVE=y

# Deliberately NOT setting CONFIG_HEAP_ABORT_WHEN_ALLOCATION_FAILS. It sounds
# like it belongs in a debug profile, but it aborts when an allocation FAILS --
# it has nothing to do with corruption detection, which poisoning already panics
# on by itself. Turning it on would abort instead of returning NULL, which would
# skip exactly the graceful out-of-memory paths worth exercising: draw_qr()'s
# fallback to a printed key when the ~19 KB canvas cannot be allocated, and
# HttpServer's detached-thread spawn backing off under heap pressure. A debug
# build should exercise those, not bypass them.
